@title TLS certificate rotation runbook
@subtitle Edge proxies: 18 proxies, 12 hostnames
@author Platform Security, Runbook owner

kpi Rotate at | 30 days left | CERT_EXPIRY_30D alert | neutral
kpi Hostnames | 12 | covered by these certificates | neutral
kpi Error limit | 2% | handshake errors on any proxy | bad

# When to rotate
Rotate a certificate when it has 30 days or fewer left. The CERT_EXPIRY_30D alert announces this. The certificates cover 12 hostnames.

# Before you start
- Open the change record.
- Confirm that no deploy is running in #deploys.
- Check that the internal CA is healthy.

# Rotation steps
!!! Never delete the old private key before step 6.

1. Confirm expiry
-- a) Run `openssl x509 -enddate -noout` on the live certificate.
-- b) Compare the date with the alert.
2. Request the new certificate
-- a) Run `certctl request --profile edge-prod`.
-- b) Check that the output lists all 12 hostnames.
3. Stage it on the canary proxy only
-- a) Copy the new certificate to `/etc/edge/certs/next` on edge-canary-01.
-- b) Reload the proxy on edge-canary-01.
4. Verify the canary
-- a) Run `openssl s_client -connect edge-canary-01:443` and check that it shows the new expiry date.
-- b) Keep handshake errors under 0.1% for 20 minutes.
5. Roll out to the other 17 proxies
-- a) Work in batches of 3.
-- b) Wait 5 minutes between batches.
6. Revoke the old certificate
-- a) Wait 48 hours, then revoke the old certificate.
-- b) Record the revocation in the change record.

# Rollback
The previous certificate stays in `/etc/edge/certs/previous` for 7 days. To roll back, copy it back into place and reload the proxy.

# Escalation
- Page Platform Security if any proxy shows more than 2% handshake errors.
- Post in #platform-sec.
